stocks-analytics-engine
======================

Overview
--------

This is a Node.js quantitative stock analytics engine that:

- Builds a deduplicated universe of tickers from Excel worksheets (Portfolio, Watchlist, index sheets).
- Fetches fundamentals from Finviz, prices from Yahoo Finance (yahoo-finance2), and institutional activity from the NASDAQ API.
- Computes cross-universe percentile signals (fundamental and technical) and a weighted composite score per ticker.
- Writes color-coded results and snapshots to an Excel workbook (`data/stocks.xlsx`) and generates a self-contained HTML report (`data/report.html`).
- Provides a protected server to serve reports and trigger analytics runs (Firebase auth / RBAC middleware).

This README.ME documents project layout, setup, environment variables, run scripts, and security notes for the `feature/googleauth-security-hardening-clean` branch.

Highlights (what this branch contains)
------------------------------------

- Main analytics pipeline: `index.js` (reads sheet lists, fetches data, computes factors, writes scores via `sheets/writeSheet.js`).
- Workbook management: `scripts/createWorkbook.js` and `scripts/cleanWorkbook.js` (idempotent creation + safe clean/regenerate flow).
- Index screener: `scripts/screenIndex.js` (Finviz scraping + filters + alpha ranking → writes index sheets).
- Visual reports: `visualizer.js` / `visualizer-analytics.js` → `data/report.html` and `data/analytics.html`.
- Protected report server: `server/serveReport.js` with Firebase-backed `authMiddleware` and `requireRole` guard.
- Tools for local testing and Firebase admin token generation in `tools/`.

Quick Start
-----------

1. Install dependencies:

   npm install

2. Configure tickers (single source of truth):

   Edit `data/tickers.json` to set `portfolio` and `watchlist` arrays.

3. Create the workbook (one-time or whenever tickers.json changes):

   node scripts/createWorkbook.js

4. Set environment variables (see Environment section), then run the analytics engine:

   npm start

   This runs the pipeline: fetch fundamentals/prices/institutional data, computes scores, writes `ScoresCurrent`, `SignalsTriggered`, and snapshots `ScoresPreviousDay`. After the run the `visualizer` will generate the HTML report.

Key Scripts
-----------

- `npm start` — runs `node index.js && node visualizer.js` to compute scores and generate the web report.
- `npm run setup` — create workbook and run `scripts/screenIndex.js all` to populate index sheets.
- `npm run clean-workbook` — deletes and regenerates `data/stocks.xlsx` from `data/tickers.json` (useful when invalid tickers accumulate).
- `npm run screen-<name>` — run index screener for `rut`, `dji`, `ndx`, `sp100`, or `all`.
- `npm run visualize` / `npm run visualize:serve` — create the report and optionally serve it locally.

Environment & Configuration
---------------------------

This project uses `dotenv`. Create a `.env` file in the project root (not checked into source control) and set the following keys as needed:

- `FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON` — a JSON string containing your Firebase service account credentials. The branch uses `firebase/admin.js` which expects this JSON string to be available in the environment; the code parses it and uses `private_key.replace(/\\n/g, "\n")` when constructing the cert.
- (Optional) Any other keys used by your local setup. `index.js` and many scripts import `dotenv/config` and depend on conventional `process.env` values.

Example `.env` snippet (shell-escaped):

# For shells: set the entire JSON string, ensure newlines are escaped
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON='{"type":"service_account", ... "private_key":"-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\\n...\\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----\n" }'

Firebase / Authentication
-------------------------

The branch includes Firebase-based authentication and RBAC middleware used by the server endpoints in `server/serveReport.js`.

- Service account: `firebase/admin.js` constructs Admin SDK credentials by parsing `FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON`.
- Token verification: `firebase/authMiddleware.js` expects an HTTP `Authorization: Bearer <idToken>` header and verifies it via `adminAuth().verifyIdToken(idToken)`.
- Tools: `tools/getUserToken.js` and `tools/get-Custom-token.js` create/print custom tokens using the Admin SDK for local testing.

Notes & Security
---------------

- Do NOT commit service-account JSON or any credentials into git. Use environment variables or a secrets manager.
- Production: prefer a secure secrets store (Vault, cloud secrets manager, GitHub Actions secrets) instead of embedding JSON in `.env`.
- Rate limits: the code implements exponential backoff (with Retry-After support) and a 1.2s inter-request delay for Finviz scraping. Respect rate limits and use retries carefully.
- The RBAC resolver logic is partially stubbed out in `firebase/authMiddleware.js` — the code demonstrates token verification and expected `req.user` attachment. If you enable the RBAC resolver, ensure it's connected to your canonical user store.

Project Layout (short)
----------------------

- `index.js` — main pipeline that builds the universe and computes rows written to the workbook.
- `data/` — API clients and static report HTML: `fundamentals.js`, `prices.js`, `institutions.js`, `report.html`, `analytics.html`.
- `factors/` — scoring functions (fundamentals, technicals, normalize, risk).
- `sheets/` — `readSheet.js` and `writeSheet.js` handling Excel interactions via `exceljs`.
- `scripts/` — setup, cleanup, and Finviz screener scripts.
- `server/` — `serveReport.js` provides authenticated routes to serve the HTML report and an admin run-report API.
- `tools/` — ad-hoc helpers and local dev utilities (token generation, tests).

Development Tips
----------------

- Use `tools/test_ticker.js` and `tools/test_invalid_ticker.js` to verify your network access and fetch logic for a single ticker.
- When adding many tickers at once, prefer modifying `data/tickers.json` then running `node scripts/createWorkbook.js` (idempotent) and `npm start`.
- If you see failures from invalid tickers, run `npm run clean-workbook` to recreate a fresh workbook from `tickers.json`.
- Linting/formatting: `npm run lint`, `npm run format`.

Troubleshooting
---------------

- If Finviz or Nasdaq calls return 429: wait, respect Retry-After, and avoid aggressively parallelizing requests. The code already includes backoff logic.
- If Firebase token verification fails: verify `FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON` is correct and the Admin SDK credentials match your Firebase project.
- Excel file location: `sheets/readSheet.js` and `sheets/writeSheet.js` expect the workbook at `data/stocks.xlsx`. Use `scripts/createWorkbook.js` to create or repair it.

Contributing
------------

Feel free to open a PR on the main branch. For anything involving credentials, environment setup, or CI/CD changes, provide a secure plan (don't commit secrets). If you'd like, I can also help prepare a GitHub Actions workflow to run the analytics and publish the generated report to GitHub Pages.

